WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.820
With The Mandalorian's second chapter, we
dive a little deeper than ever before into

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:08.360
the state of galaxy immediately following
the fall of the Empire.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.980
While a lot has changed, some things stay
the same.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.280
Here are a few of the best callbacks.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:19.029
By the beginning of Chapter Two, The Mandalorian
is trying to get "the asset" a creature that

00:00:19.029 --> 00:00:23.829
appears to be some kind of adorable baby Yoda
back to his mysterious client, a remnant of

00:00:23.829 --> 00:00:27.020
the crumbling Empire, but it's a treacherous
and violent journey.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:31.720
This trip includes a run-in with Jawas, a
race of creature very recognizable to Star

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:36.140
Wars fans, visually distinct because of their
hooded cloaks, short stature, and glowing

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:36.640
eyes.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.300
And also their tendency to steal anything
that isn't nailed down.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.879
After they strip down his ship, the Razor
Crest, the Mandalorian blasts any Jawas that

00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:52.140
get in his path on a violent mission to get
his stuff back, turning them into dust as

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.880
they poof away without a trace.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.719
Later on, Kuiil remarks to the Mandalorian
that the creatures "really don't like him

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:58.400
for some reason."

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.720
The Mandalorian replies, "Well, I did disintegrate
a few of them."

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:06.500
This subtly calls back to a brief exchange
in The Empire Strikes Back between Darth Vader

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.880
and a collection of dicey-looking bounty hunters,
in which the Sith lord tells them that he

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:15.220
wants the crew of the Millennium Falcon brought
to him alive, by any means necessary.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:19.860
And addressing Boba Fett, Vader makes a point
of specifying "No disintegrations."

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.460
In other words, these Mandalorian bounty hunters
just love blasting their enemies into paste

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.980
and are probably known to do so when given
the chance.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.960
And while we're talking about fancy Mandalorian
weapons, it's pretty nice to see that wrist-mounted

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.440
flamethrower make an appearance.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.260
Can jetpacks be too far behind?

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.700
While the full identity of "the asset" is
not yet clear by the end of the second chapter

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:45.409
of The Mandalorian, it's obvious to anyone
with even a passing familiarity of Star Wars

00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:48.720
that the little guy is a very young version
of a Yoda-type alien.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.520
probably designed to be a best-selling toy
this Christmas.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.219
While this creature-with-no-name is reportedly
50 years old, he's still baby-like in many

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:00.160
ways... but at the same time, very wise and
Yoda-like in others.

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:04.580
Specifically, he can definitely use the Force,
even without apparently going through years

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.020
of fancy Jedi training.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.280
He lifts a gigantic Mud Horn off the ground
with a bit of effort, not unlike that time

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.980
when Yoda thought real hard and lifted Luke's
X-wing right out of the swamps of Dagobah.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:19.640
The Force takes many forms, including healing
abilities, and this tiny Yoda successor may

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.400
also have that power, as seen when he attempts
to heal The Mandalorian's wound.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.620
The great Obi-Wan Kenobi may have used this
ability to help Luke recover from the Tusken

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:31.640
Raider attack in A New Hope, but the ability
to heal with the Force has been a bit iffy

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.760
and weirdly defined in Star Wars canon…
until now.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.430
And it also begs the question are Yoda's people
just naturally super-strong with the Force?

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.020
It's hard to tell under all of the special
effects that make him look like an Ugnaught,

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:49.380
but Kuiil the Mandalorian's ally, friend,
and lifesaver is portrayed by three-time Oscar-nominated

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:54.860
actor Nick Nolte, who brings his specific
world-weary style of acting to the loner alien.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.840
But really, Kuiil doesn't have to work alone
if he doesn't want to.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.260
After they've escaped a whole lot of trouble
in Chapter Two of the series, The Mandalorian

00:03:02.261 --> 00:03:04.870
suggests making their relationship a bit more
permanent.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:09.380
He offers Kuiil a well-paid spot on his crew,
but Kuiil respectfully rejects it.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.980
"I am honored, but I have worked a lifetime
to finally be free of servitude."

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:20.080
That's a weighty statement, but his reasoning
can be traced back to The Empire Strikes Back

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:21.700
and a visit to Cloud City.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:26.420
Ugnaughts were briefly depicted as the industrious
backbone of that society, hired because of

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:31.210
their work ethic and given a home after their
own planet was decimated by slavery, according

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.760
to official Star Wars lore.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:36.980
As a man who was once without a home in the
galaxy, leaving behind his hard-earned patch

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.420
of land just doesn't seem like the better
option.

